    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Act as an expert frontend developer and data visualization specialist. I need you to write a complete, single-file HTML prototype (including embedded CSS and JavaScript) for a data dashboard tracking the Bolivian economy. \n\n**Tech Stack:**\n* **HTML5 & CSS:** Use Tailwind CSS via CDN for clean, modern, and responsive styling.\n* **JavaScript:** Use vanilla JS.\n* **Data Parsing:** Use PapaParse (via CDN) to fetch and parse the external CSV files.\n* **Charting:** Use Chart.js or Plotly.js (via CDN) for interactive, responsive graphs.\n\n**Data Sources (Fetch directly via these URLs):**\n*Synthetic Data:*\n* National: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/national/supermarket_1_tracker_results.csv\n* La Paz: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/la_paz/supermarket_1_tracker_results.csv\n* Cochabamba: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/cochabamba/supermarket_1_tracker_results.csv\n* Santa Cruz: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/santa_cruz/supermarket_1_tracker_results.csv\n* Live Product Count: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/supermarket_1_daily_n_counts.csv\n\n*Official Benchmark Data:*\n* National Official: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/config/National%20CPI%20By%20Category.csv\n* La Paz Official: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/config/La%20Paz%20Official%20Eq.csv\n* Cochabamba Official: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/config/Cochabamba%20Official%20Eq.csv\n* Santa Cruz Official: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/config/Santa%20Cruz%20Official%20Eq.csv\n\n**UI Layout & Features:**\n1.  **Header:** Title the page \"Bolivia Synthetic CPI Tracker\". Include a brief methodology tooltip or text block explaining that the synthetic index uses a matched-model Jevons calculation and explicitly filters out volatile *temporada* (seasonal) categories to prevent downward bias.\n2.  **Navigation/Tabs:** Create a tabbed interface or distinct sections to easily navigate between the different views without cluttering the screen.\n3.  **Graph 1 (National View):** A line chart showing the National CPI over time. Add UI toggles (checkboxes or a dropdown) allowing the user to overlay the La Paz, Cochabamba, and Santa Cruz synthetic data on this same graph.\n4.  **Graphs 2, 3, & 4 (City Views):** Individual line charts for La Paz, Cochabamba, and Santa Cruz. Each graph must display the city\'s top-line CPI and allow the user to toggle on/off specific category-level CPIs for that city.\n5.  **Benchmark Overlay:** Every CPI graph (National and City-specific) must have a prominent toggle button to overlay the respective \"Official Data\" line for direct comparison. \n6.  **Graph 5 (Product Tracking):** A stacked area chart or line graph showing the live product count categorized by type over time.\n7.  **Data Export:** Below each graph, include a \"Download CSV\" button that allows users to download the exact data currently being rendered in that specific chart.\n\nEnsure the code is robust enough to handle asynchronous data fetching, and add loading spinners to the charts while the CSVs are being parsed."
    items {
      text: "Act as an expert frontend developer and data visualization specialist. I need you to write a complete, single-file HTML prototype (including embedded CSS and JavaScript) for a data dashboard tracking the Bolivian economy. \n\n**Tech Stack:**\n* **HTML5 & CSS:** Use Tailwind CSS via CDN for clean, modern, and responsive styling.\n* **JavaScript:** Use vanilla JS.\n* **Data Parsing:** Use PapaParse (via CDN) to fetch and parse the external CSV files.\n* **Charting:** Use Chart.js or Plotly.js (via CDN) for interactive, responsive graphs.\n\n**Data Sources (Fetch directly via these URLs):**\n*Synthetic Data:*\n* National: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/national/supermarket_1_tracker_results.csv\n* La Paz: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/la_paz/supermarket_1_tracker_results.csv\n* Cochabamba: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/cochabamba/supermarket_1_tracker_results.csv\n* Santa Cruz: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/santa_cruz/supermarket_1_tracker_results.csv\n* Live Product Count: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/results/supermarket_1/supermarket_1_daily_n_counts.csv\n\n*Official Benchmark Data:*\n* National Official: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/config/National%20CPI%20By%20Category.csv\n* La Paz Official: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/config/La%20Paz%20Official%20Eq.csv\n* Cochabamba Official: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/config/Cochabamba%20Official%20Eq.csv\n* Santa Cruz Official: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thomasriveros/robust-cpi-bolivia/refs/heads/main/config/Santa%20Cruz%20Official%20Eq.csv\n\n**UI Layout & Features:**\n1.  **Header:** Title the page \"Bolivia Synthetic CPI Tracker\". Include a brief methodology tooltip or text block explaining that the synthetic index uses a matched-model Jevons calculation and explicitly filters out volatile *temporada* (seasonal) categories to prevent downward bias.\n2.  **Navigation/Tabs:** Create a tabbed interface or distinct sections to easily navigate between the different views without cluttering the screen.\n3.  **Graph 1 (National View):** A line chart showing the National CPI over time. Add UI toggles (checkboxes or a dropdown) allowing the user to overlay the La Paz, Cochabamba, and Santa Cruz synthetic data on this same graph.\n4.  **Graphs 2, 3, & 4 (City Views):** Individual line charts for La Paz, Cochabamba, and Santa Cruz. Each graph must display the city\'s top-line CPI and allow the user to toggle on/off specific category-level CPIs for that city.\n5.  **Benchmark Overlay:** Every CPI graph (National and City-specific) must have a prominent toggle button to overlay the respective \"Official Data\" line for direct comparison. \n6.  **Graph 5 (Product Tracking):** A stacked area chart or line graph showing the live product count categorized by type over time.\n7.  **Data Export:** Below each graph, include a \"Download CSV\" button that allows users to download the exact data currently being rendered in that specific chart.\n\nEnsure the code is robust enough to handle asynchronous data fetching, and add loading spinners to the charts while the CSVs are being parsed."
    }
  }
}
