  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Calculating Inflation Dynamics**\n\nOkay, I\'m integrating a new inflation rate section now. I\'ve got the framework set up to calculate Year-over-Year, Month-over-Month, and Day-over-Day inflation rates dynamically from the CPI data displayed in both the National View and City View components. This requires adjusting the existing data processing logic to support the inflation calculations.\n\n\n**Implementing Inflation Graphs**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing the daily inflation graphs beneath the CPI data. I\'m structuring them to display Year-over-Year, Month-over-Month, and Day-over-Day inflation rates, dependent on the user\'s CPI data selections. I\'ve realized YOY won\'t be immediately populated because our synthetic data only goes back to July 2024, so initial YOY graphs might be empty.\n\n\n**Refining Inflation Calculation Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the inflation calculation logic, specifically addressing the Daily-over-Day (DOD) rate, and I\'m realizing DOD is not undefined if the data are truly daily. I\'m focusing on creating a generic utility function to compute inflation for any dataset, handling missing data points by finding the closest prior value exactly N days ago. We\'ll need to handle gaps in the daily data when calculating DOD. The user\'s request for daily YOY, MOM, and DOD rates is now the focus.\n\n\n**Optimizing Inflation Date References**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a robust approach for finding reference dates to calculate inflation. My focus is on handling potential gaps in the daily CPI data, specifically weekend gaps. I\'m moving away from date offsets to a dateNum comparison to more accurately locate the necessary data points, accounting for potential data discontinuities. I\'m structuring a `calculateInflation` helper function to handle different periods - DOD, MOM, and YOY - and different metrics.\n\n\n**Defining Historical Data Retrieval**\n\nI\'m now writing the logic to retrieve historical CPI data accurately for inflation calculations. I am prioritizing a robust method for getting prior period values, allowing for missing data, especially for Month-over-Month (MOM) calculations. I\'ve designed a utility that now searches backward in the dataset, handling daily, monthly, and yearly intervals. I will use a tolerance of 3 days to account for weekends.\n\n\n**Refining Data Matching Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the helper to find the most appropriate prior data value, particularly dealing with monthly CPI data. I realized that for MOM calculations with official data, the previous month\'s value is the closest match, even if the target date isn\'t exactly the 1st of the month, which our function handles. For DOD, since official data is monthly, the previous month\'s value is the only possible value to use. I\'m setting a threshold of four days to ensure a valid match, adapting it to handle synthetic daily data.\n\n\n**Adjusting Day-Over-Day Thresholds**\n\nI\'m currently adjusting the Day-over-Day calculation logic for official data, setting a threshold of five days to determine if DOD is meaningful. If the date difference is greater than this, the DOD will be null to avoid misinterpretations. This requires an update to the getHistoricalValue function.\n\n\n**Expanding Data Retrieval Tolerance**\n\nI\'m now expanding the tolerance for date matching in the `getHistoricalValue` function, especially for monthly data, setting it to 15 days. For official data that is monthly, the previous month\'s value is the closest match to compare. This considers different month lengths (like February) and handles variations in date alignment for calculations like Month-over-Month. Year-over-Year remains empty for data back to July.\n\n\n**Implementing Inflation Display**\n\nI\'m now focusing on displaying inflation rates based on user selection of DoD, MoM, or YoY, integrating it into the `NationalView` and `CityView` components. I\'ve designed the inflation data calculation using `useMemo` to efficiently compute inflation for each metric in the dataset. I will be sure to handle cases where historical data is missing or zero, and will show null when rates cannot be calculated.\n\n```\nconst [inflationPeriod, setInflationPeriod] = useState<\'DOD\' | \'MOM\' | \'YOY\'>(\'MOM\');\n```\n\n\n**Reusing Chart Component**\n\nI\'m now integrating inflation rate charts directly into the existing UI, leveraging the `Line` components I\'ve built for the CPI data visualization. I\'ve configured the inflation charts to display data based on the selected inflation period: DoD, MoM, or YoY, with a toggle. I\'ve added conditional logic to the tooltip so that percentage formatting is only displayed when appropriate. I\'ll pass a `isPercentage` prop to `CustomTooltip`. I will also add an axis formatter for the inflation graph.\n\n\n**Clarifying MOM Inflation Logic**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the Month-over-Month inflation calculation, specifically for synthetic daily data versus official monthly data. I\'ve realized pure daily comparisons for official data (which are monthly) lead to noisy results. For official monthly data, the MoM calculation is straightforward: `current_month / last_month - 1`. For synthetic data, a rolling 30-day calculation is appropriate, like `synthetic_Aug_15 / synthetic_Jul_16 - 1`.\n\n\n**Calculating Rolling Inflation**\n\nI\'m now writing logic for the rolling inflation calculations based on daily data, even though official data is monthly. I\'m focusing on \"daily basis\" requirement from the user, even for official data. I\'m writing `getHistoricalValue` to calculate rolling Day-over-Day, Month-over-Month (30-day), and Year-over-Year (365-day) for every daily data point in the dataset.\n\n\n**Refining Tolerance Calculation**\n\nI\'m now refining the data matching tolerance within `getHistoricalValue`, specifically for Day-over-Day calculations. After reexamining the logic, I realize that DOD for official monthly data is not meaningful and 