    thinking: "**Addressing Token Limits**\n\nI\'m currently grappling with token limits in generating responses and edits, specifically when adding the inflation graph below the CPI graph on both NationalView and CityView pages. The core task is to integrate these dynamic inflation graphs that update based on user input, but the token constraints are proving a significant hurdle in the current implementation. I need to find a workaround for this.\n\n\n**Calculating Inflation Data**\n\nI\'ve been working on inflation calculations based on the CPI data. I\'m focusing on providing options for Day-over-Day, Month-over-Month, and Year-over-Year inflation on a daily basis. The formulas are now clear, and the challenge is handling data availability, especially since the dataset starts in July 2024. I\'m building a function to calculate these inflation metrics, and considering integration into the `chartData` hook.\n\n\n**Refining Inflation Calculation**\n\nI\'m now refining the inflation calculation function. I\'m focusing on dynamic calculation based on user selections. I\'m also ensuring the function handles various inflation modes like Day-over-Day, Month-over-Month, and Year-over-Year calculations, including the logic for offsetting the dates correctly. I\'m using TypeScript types to ensure data consistency, and considering both linear and binary search for finding past data points.\n\n\n**Clarifying Data Granularity**\n\nI\'ve realized a critical issue. The official CPI data is monthly, not daily, making daily inflation calculations tricky. If I simply use the previous data point for \"Day-over-Day\" inflation, it will actually reflect a monthly change. I\'m reassessing how to accurately represent daily inflation metrics given the monthly data resolution and user requirement for daily granularity. I must consider how to best extrapolate or present the data, because the initial approach won\'t work.\n\n\n**Implementing Data Lookup**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `getPastValue` function to retrieve historical CPI data, using a backward search. I\'m focusing on handling different time offsets for Day-over-Day, Month-over-Month, and Year-over-Year calculations, considering a maximum tolerance to account for potential data gaps. I\'m also ensuring the function efficiently handles potential missing values and stops the search when a tolerance limit is hit. I\'m prioritizing accuracy based on exact date offsets in the calculation.\n\n\n**Defining Date Offsets**\n\nI\'ve finalized the date offsets for inflation calculations: 1 day for Day-over-Day, 30 days for Month-over-Month, and 365 days for Year-over-Year, or 365.25 if the time delta is very large. I\'ve set tolerances of 2 days for DOD, 4 days for MOM, and 5 days for YOY. I\'m starting the implementation within `NationalView`, considering a reusable `InflationChart` component or inline rendering based on user needs, for toggling and data computation. I\'m focusing on inflation mode and active keys like `Synthetic_National`, etc. and use a `useMemo` hook to manage the data.\n\n\n**Defining Inflation Logic**\n\nI\'m now defining the `calculateInflationData` function. I\'m focusing on handling different inflation modes (Day-over-Day, Month-over-Month, Year-over-Year), and the offsets and tolerances, considering the monthly nature of the CPI data. I\'m aiming for efficient data lookup within the function for accurate inflation calculations.\n\n\n**Refining Inflation Data**\n\nI\'ve been refining the inflation data handling, and now I\'m considering scenarios where missing data occurs. Specifically, when `connectNulls` is true, I expect the inflation calculation should also treat the nulls in an equivalent manner. Also, I\'ve realized, if the data is monthly, MOM calculations are only going to output inflation data on the 1st of each month! So I can \"connect\" those dots for the user.\n\n\n**Finalizing Inflation Logic**\n\nI\'ve got the inflation data logic nailed down and I\'m quite satisfied. Now the `calculateInflationData` function includes a very accurate DOD calculation. Nulls will be handled with connected lines, as expected. The official DOD will be omitted since there\'s no data, which is correct. I\'ve considered that MOM inflation data will naturally occur only on the 1st of each month. I\'ve refined the logic and offsets.\n\n\n**Implementing Inflation Toggle**\n\nI\'m now integrating the inflation mode toggle (DOD, MOM, YOY) into both `NationalView` and `CityView`, using a state variable. I\'m focusing on extracting the `calculateInflationData` logic into a reusable function to avoid duplication. This reusable function ensures consistent inflation calculations across views, while the toggle provides dynamic control over the inflation metric displayed. My objective is to deliver a smooth and configurable experience for the user.\n\n\n**Rendering Inflation Charts**\n\nI\'m now focusing on rendering the inflation chart by either duplicating the existing CPI chart\'s structure or adding a new `ResponsiveContainer` and `LineChart`. I\'ll use the `calculateInflationData` function to compute the necessary inflation data and integrate it with the chart rendering logic. I\'ve re-reviewed the `NationalView` return and realized a possible token limit error was due to long response text.\n\n\n**Implementing Multi-Edits**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `multi_edit_file` edits to seamlessly integrate the inflation data calculations and charts. I\'m starting by adding the `calculateInflationData` function, and then I\'ll integrate it into `NationalView` and `CityView`, adding state for the `inflationMode`, computing the `activeKeys` and `inflationData` to render the chart below. I\'m considering dynamic `activeCategories` in `CityView` for a smooth implementation. I\'ll place the new function below the `exportCSV` utility.\n\n\n**Adding Inflation Utility**\n\nI\'m now integrating the finalized `calculateInflationData` function, along with appropriate types. I\'m focusing on ensuring this utility is called as a separate tool, keeping the code chunks smaller. I\'m prioritizing accuracy in my implementation.\n\n\n"
