  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Versioning Strategies**\n\nI\'m currently evaluating different versioning strategies for cache invalidation. Adding a timestamp query parameter like `?v=${Date.now()}` is one approach, but I\'m also exploring using a daily cache with `?v=${new Date().toISOString().split(\'T\')[0]}`. The key is to ensure the user always fetches the latest content from the main branch. I\'ll need to figure out which strategy is most robust.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "Yes, they will! Because the URLs use the `refs/heads/main` path on `raw.githubusercontent.com`, they point directly to the tip of your main branch. \n\nAny time a user loads or refreshes the web app, it will fetch the newest data you have pushed to GitHub. \n\n**One small detail to keep in mind:** \nGitHub\'s `raw` content delivery network (CDN) caches files for about **5 minutes**. So when you push a new update to GitHub, it might take up to 5 minutes before the new data actually shows up on the live dashboard.\n\nIf you ever notice that a browser is aggressively caching old data even after 5 minutes, we can add a \"cache-buster\" (like appending `?t=timestamp` to the end of the URL fetches) to force the browser to always pull the freshest data \342\200\223 just let me know if you\'d like me to add that!"
