    thinking: "**Designing the Toggle**\n\nI\'m currently focused on implementing a language toggle within the application\'s header. My initial thought is a straightforward button, switching between \"Espa\303\261ol\" and \"English\". The next step involves integrating this button with an `App.tsx` file by adding the \'lang\' state.\n\n\n**Refining the State**\n\nI\'ve decided to manage language state at the top level for contextual browsing. I\'ve defined a `Lang` type for \'en\' and \'es\', and I\'m updating the `App.tsx` file with this state. Now, I\'m working on the data structure for translated text to minimize redundancy.\n\n\n**Translating the Content**\n\nMy current focus is on translating the application\'s content. I\'m now incorporating translated text for various elements, including city names like \"Cochabamba\" and \"Santa Cruz\", product counts, and methodologies. I\'m also addressing text for loading states and national CPI trends, striving for consistent and accurate translations across all sections, especially the city-specific CPI breakdowns.\n\n\n**Expanding the Translations**\n\nI\'m now expanding the Spanish translations to include text for the \"Live Product Count\" section, methodology descriptions, and the forward-fill date label. I\'m focusing on ensuring accuracy and clarity for each element. The goal is a comprehensive and user-friendly experience in both languages. I\'m also ensuring the title in Spanish is incorporated.\n\n\n**Interpreting the Data**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specific wording for the methodology section and the national trends title, working to clearly explain that this isn\'t a complete CPI and uses a \"Core-5\" basket. My aim is to make it transparent that this is an alternative measure for tracking retail price trends, and isn\'t a macro substitute. The goal is clarity and accurate representation of the data. I\'m focusing on ensuring accuracy in the translated labels for the tabs.\n\n\n**Refining the Translations**\n\nI\'m now integrating more Spanish translations, focusing on the refined data labels, specifically for city breakdowns and data overlays. I\'m ensuring all aspects, like the \"Core-5\" explanation and the overlay of official data, are accurate and clear in Spanish, especially the key phrases like \"\303\215ndice sint\303\251tico\" and \"Tasa de Inflaci\303\263n\".\n\n\n**Implementing the Context**\n\nI\'m now considering the implementation of the translation context. I\'m thinking of passing the `lang` state to each component and then computing `t` inside them. This makes it easier, but I think an even better approach would be to utilize context for more elegant data management. I\'m exploring how to update `NationalView`, `CityView`, `ProductCountView`, and `MethodologyView` to accept `t: typeof T[\'en\']`.\n\n\n"
