  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Testing Unparse Functionality**\n\nI\'m currently experimenting with how `Papa.unparse` handles iterative key additions to objects. Specifically, I\'m checking what happens when I introduce new keys that aren\'t initially present, or when keys exist with null values in the starting object. The goal is to fully understand its behavior in edge cases. This testing process should help me better predict Papa\'s performance in real world use-cases. I will then test the inflation data.\n\n\n**Analyzing Data Processing Pipeline**\n\nI\'ve been examining how inflation data is calculated and how that impacts the final output of the `Papa.unparse` function. Specifically, I\'ve realized that the `calculateInflationData` function processes only the keys provided in the `activeKeys` parameter. This means the output data, which is then unparsed, includes original data plus inflation data only for the selected active keys. This also means if the `activeKeys` is \"core 5\", then the output will include `core 5` and `core 5_Inflation`, so `Papa.unparse` will output all.\n\n\n**Clarifying Download Requirements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the user\'s explicit request for the download format. The user wants the download to contain *only* the selected CPI and inflation measures, which suggests I need to rethink the current export behavior. Previously, I thought the download included all raw CSV fields alongside inflation data for the active series, leading to potentially messy outputs. Now, I\'m focusing on ensuring the unparsed CSV output accurately reflects the user\'s selections. I need to verify that `Papa.unparse` isn\'t using only the first row and that it properly handles a mix of data with and without inflation values.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 757207229
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
